<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> How is all this affected by multi-tasking?
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> Aren't the constructs you need for that appropriate in a 
single-task environment as well?
<[Robert] R.BERKEY> Each task must have separate stacks and stack pointers.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> Each task must have its own context, as well as sharing 
some global context.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> The question is whether or not something like DO...LOOP is 
a form of
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> other-taskness.
<[Robert] R.BERKEY> I don't see how.
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> Interesting idea, Doug.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> That is, it has some state associated with it that is 
independant of the
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> state of the task that spawned it.  This is working its way 
back around
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> to closures/continuations again.  One of the things that 
bugs me about
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> But, isn't that true of any flow of control construct, 
even IF .THEN  ??
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> DO...LOOP is the restrictions on access to the index 
variable and the ways
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> in which you can/can't abort it.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> If then isn't quite the same, because the state is inherent 
in the fact that
<[Robert] R.BERKEY> Right, there is something wrong with it.
